* Frozen netlist snapshot for record 20260801-034142-c26da47
* source     : sim/amp-loop-stability/testbench/tb_loop_stability.spice
* sha256     : 2c59a8b8bdf2cea82f499ef290624b09ffb0b68e779f8bc03a4d0eed164e38eb
* This is a verbatim copy taken at record time. Do not edit.
* amp-loop-stability -- issue #10 loop-gain / stability-margin sim
*
* Netlist FRAGMENT for sim/harness (sim/README.md format). The harness
* supplies .include of the gf180mcu models, the corner .lib sections,
* .temp, the .control block and .end. Parameters available: vdd_val,
* vdd_nom, temp_c.
*
* Content below is design/netlist/bandgap_core.spice and
* design/netlist/bandgap_amp.spice (the #10 offset-budgeted amp, see
* design/bandgap_error_budget.md), stripped of xschem comment lines and
* re-wrapped as .subckt/.ends, followed by a loop-gain probe.
*
* METHODOLOGY -- single-injection loop gain with the exact correction
* (Middlebrook 1975 / Rosenstark): the servo loop closes through exactly
* one wire in this topology (the amp's "out" pin directly drives the
* core's "fb" net, the four mirror gates -- see
* design/bandgap_operating_point.md Sec 1). Because the break point is a
* single conductor (one voltage difference, one shared branch current),
* NOT a general two-port disconnect, a single series test-voltage
* injection gives the loop gain EXACTLY (no approximation, and Tian/
* Middlebrook dual injection is provably equivalent to this single
* injection for a 1-port break -- dual injection exists to handle the
* harder general 2-port case, which does not arise here).
*
* Derivation: let B = fb_gates (feeds the forward path: core -> amp),
* A = amp_out (the forward path's own result, in the closed loop A=B).
* Break the wire and insert an ideal AC test source: B = A + vt (vt=1<0,
* DC=0, so the DC operating point is byte-for-byte identical to the
* closed loop -- no discontinuity). The forward path itself is
* A = G(B) for whatever G this circuit implements, so
* A = G(A + vt) => A(1-G) = G*vt => B = A + vt => B/vt = 1/(1-G).
* Measuring x = V(fb_gates)/vt (=V(fb_gates) since vt=1) gives the loop
* gain EXACTLY via T = 1 - 1/x (algebra: x=1/(1-G) => 1/x=1-G => G=1-1/x,
* and T=G, the return ratio, satisfies the closed-loop characteristic
* equation 1-T=0 exactly -- this is pure algebra from the B=A+vt wiring,
* no sign assumption about the circuit anywhere in the derivation).
*
* CRITICAL POINT, NOT THE TEXTBOOK -180 DEGREES: because the closed-loop
* pole condition here is 1-T=0 (T=+1 danger), not the classic op-amp
* "Vout=A*(Vin-beta*Vout)" form (1+T=0, T=-1 danger), the Nyquist danger
* point for THIS T is magnitude=1 AND phase=0 (mod 360 degrees) -- not
* magnitude=1 AND phase=180. Measured at nominal PVT this circuit's T has
* DC phase ~180 degrees (a real, physical extra inversion from the R2-
* mediated asymmetry between the sns1/sns2 branches -- confirmed by
* simulation, not assumed), and |T(f)| does not cross 0dB in the
* classical single-crossing sense: DC gain ~42dB rolls off to a local
* minimum (worst case ~0.09dB at bjt_ss/-40C/2.97V) then RISES again to a
* high-frequency plateau (a parasitic Cgd feedthrough path around the
* single gain stage). Throughout this whole sweep, T's phase stays in a
* band roughly 80-205 degrees -- nowhere near the true danger phase of 0
* (mod 360) -- so the Nyquist locus never approaches the critical point
* (+1,0) regardless of how thin the magnitude margin looks in isolation.
*
* STABILITY CRITERION (this design's own choice, not a ratified spec
* line -- see design/bandgap_error_budget.md Sec 4 for the full
* justification and the sign-convention derivation above, including the
* toy-circuit cross-check that caught an initial sign error): phase
* margin, defined as the angular distance of T's phase (evaluated at the
* frequency of GLOBAL MINIMUM |T(f)| over 0.1 Hz - 2 GHz -- the worst-case
* approach to the critical point) from 0 degrees (mod 360), must stay
* >= 45 degrees at every PVT corner. The worst-case loop-gain magnitude
* at that same frequency is reported alongside for context.
*
* No startup circuit exists yet (#11): seed toward the intended nonzero-
* current state via .ic, exactly as sim/bandgap-loop-smoke does.

.subckt bandgap_core vdd sns1 sns2 vss fb vref ibias
XM1 d1 fb vdd vdd pfet_03v3 L=2u W=20u nf=1 ad='int((nf+1)/2) * W/nf * 0.18u' as='int((nf+2)/2) * W/nf * 0.18u' pd='2*int((nf+1)/2) * (W/nf + 0.18u)'
+ ps='2*int((nf+2)/2) * (W/nf + 0.18u)' nrd='0.18u / W' nrs='0.18u / W' sa=0 sb=0 sd=0 m=1
XMC1 sns1 casc d1 vdd pfet_03v3 L=2u W=20u nf=1 ad='int((nf+1)/2) * W/nf * 0.18u' as='int((nf+2)/2) * W/nf * 0.18u' pd='2*int((nf+1)/2) * (W/nf + 0.18u)'
+ ps='2*int((nf+2)/2) * (W/nf + 0.18u)' nrd='0.18u / W' nrs='0.18u / W' sa=0 sb=0 sd=0 m=1
XQ1 vss vss sns1 pnp_05p00x05p00 m=1
